[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix. Read report by report, it holds three
different bugs. Each one now has its own ticket, so a fix for one is not mistaken for a fix for all.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the first row. The other two keep coming after it ships.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} />

## How each report was placed

- **One report, one id.** The export pages overlap, so some reports appear twice. They are counted once.
- **By tenant, not by browser.** A report's cause depends on the tenant: the locale they write dates
  in and the booking form they run. Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and is
  not used here.
- **`parser`:** a day-first tenant whose date came out with day and month swapped. Most bodies
  name both dates, and each pair is an exact swap.
- **`hint`:** the same swap, but typed into the booking-v3 form, whose hint shows `MM/DD/YYYY`.
  People type what the hint says. The parser fix does not reach it.
- **`one-day`:** the date moved by one day, or differs between two people in different cities.
  That is not a swap. The desk had already noted these reports, but filed them under `parser`.

## Why one ticket hid them

The desk merges on the subject line, not the body ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
A subject line says how the problem felt ("wrong day", "dates mixed up"), not what caused it, and
most subject lines in #4471 occur under more than one of the problems above. So no subject rule
could have kept them apart. Reading the bodies and joining each report to its tenant did.

